From 1ef222f035119ddb2a2d04aa6ebffc50f5661561 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ericek111 <erik.brocko@letemsvetemapplem.eu>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 16:38:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 36/47] NMXVGAFB: hardware cursor over virtio-gpu's cursor
 queue

Every pointer move was a full WindowServer composite and flip, timed by
input, not vsync: 78 frames/s for a 120 Hz pointer circle once flips
completed at the host's latch point. The framebuffer now offers a
hardware cursor: the image (convertCursorImage to 64x64 ARGB32) lives in
a blob resource, UPDATE_CURSOR/MOVE_CURSOR go on the cursor queue (fire
and forget under a spinlock; a move finding it full is dropped). QEMU's
GTK display turns the image into the window's own pointer, so the host
draws it at the host's input rate. A transparent second blob stands for
"hidden", which GTK ignores with an absolute pointer. Images larger than
64x64 (enlarged accessibility cursors) stay in software.

Measured: 0 flips during 5 s of pointer circling (was ~600).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
